<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">The shaded band starts at the October 7 settle of $88.28. USD/CAD is plotted on its own scale, so a rising line is a weaker Canadian dollar.</p>
<p>The same $101 oil now arrives with a different set of Canadian prices. The Government of Canada 10-year yield closed Thursday at 3.83%, nine basis points below its 3.92% of September 16, and the dollar is 1.5% weaker than on that date. A month ago this oil price came with a stronger currency and a higher yield.</p>
<h2>The Rate Channel Moved on Jobs Day</h2>
<p>The sharpest recent move in Canadian yields came on October 9, when the Labour Force Survey showed 68,000 jobs lost. The 10-year fell 7 basis points to 3.86% and the 5-year fell 6 basis points to 3.54% on a day when WTI rose 0.4%. Neither yield has recovered since, even as crude gained 10.4%.</p>
