<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">The Labour Force Survey on October 9 showed 68,000 jobs lost and unemployment at 6.5%. The dashed line marks the September 16 10-year level of 3.92%; October 16 yields publish after the close.</p>
<p>Governor Macklem said on Friday that the Bank of Canada has been looking through the rise in energy prices, but that the longer elevated prices last, the harder that gets. The bond market is pricing that tension as damage to growth first. Canada holds its policy rate at 2.25% against a Fed range of 3.75% to 4.00%, and the Government of Canada 10-year yield sat 126 basis points below the U.S. 10-year at 5.09% on Thursday.</p>
<h2>Three Morning Calms Ended at Once</h2>
<p>This morning three desks described stability. WTI was below $100, the VIX had closed at 16.46 inside a 22-session range of 14.21 to 17.71, and the TSX had spent 22 sessions between closes of 35,041.90 and 36,335.60. By late Friday WTI was $101.41, the VIX had risen above 20 and was on pace for its highest close since June 10, and the S&P 500 and Nasdaq were down about 1.1% and 2.1% in an AI-led sell-off. The TSX noon print of 36,338.36 sat 2.76 points above the top of its band, led by materials and energy.</p>
<p>Canadian equities are pricing the barrels and the bullion. The dollar and the bond are pricing the economy. Monday tests which side is right when Statistics Canada publishes September CPI nine days before the October 28 decision. A hot print with oil above $100 revives October hike odds, which stood near 25% this morning, and the first place to look is the 5-year at 3.53% and the three-month bill at 2.40%. That bill is the input to the first quarter 2027 prescribed rate, which moves from 3% to 4% only if the October average clears 3.00%. If USD/CAD does not fall on a hot print, the rate channel has overridden the oil channel.</p>',
